# The toolchain, in one place. `mix kiln.toolchain.check` fails when the
# Dockerfile's ARGs or mix.exs's `elixir:` requirement disagree with this file,
# and CI's setup-beam reads it directly (version-file:) rather than restating it.

# Exact versions on purpose. CI used to pin "1.19"/"27", which setup-beam
# resolves to whatever is newest at the time — so the toolchain CI tested on
# moved on its own, and could not be matched by the Dockerfile, which needs
# exact tags. See #600.

# Floor: Elixir 1.20 (mix.exs says `~> 1.20`). The hard floor used to be 1.19.3
# — config/dev.exs uses the `E` (`:export`) regex modifier on its live-reload
# patterns, which 1.18 cannot parse (#572) — but the practical one is the
# formatter: 1.20's `mix format` breaks some lines differently from 1.19's, so a
# tree formatted on 1.20 fails a 1.19 CI's "Check formatting" and vice versa.
# One version for everyone is the only way that check means anything.

# Changing these is a real change: bump, run `mix kiln.toolchain.check`, mirror
# into the Dockerfile ARGs, and expect dialyzer to have opinions on an OTP major
# bump (#599). setup-beam matches these strictly, so a version with no published
# build for ubuntu-24.04 fails every job — check builds.hex.pm first, and Docker
# Hub for the matching `hexpm/elixir` builder tag.

# CI, the release image and a developer's machine run the same pair. They did
# not always: until 1.20.4 / OTP 29.1.1 this file held 1.19.5 / OTP 27 as a
# deliberate floor while development ran ahead on 1.20 / OTP 29 (#600). That gap
# had two specific costs. OTP 29's dialyzer performs stricter opacity checking
# than 27's, so that class of warning was invisible to CI and caught only by
# whoever ran `mix dialyzer` locally — which is how #599 got in and stayed
# green. And the formatter disagreement above failed downstream overlay PRs on
# code that was clean on the author's machine.

# Node (`nodejs`) builds the assets — `npm ci`, then esbuild/tailwind — in CI
# (setup-node reads this file via `node-version-file:`) and in the release
# image (the Dockerfile's NODE_VERSION, held to this line by the same check).
# Before it was declared here, CI said "22" and the image installed Debian's
# Node 18.

# That is also why these comments are separated by blank lines and never by a
# bare `#`. setup-node does not parse this file the way asdf does: it takes the
# first line that is a single word, or `nodejs <version>`, so a lone `#` above
# the `nodejs` line is read as Node version "#". `mix kiln.toolchain.check`
# applies setup-node's own pattern and fails if it would not land on `nodejs`.

# This file also decides what *production* runs: the release image should run
# the toolchain the test suite and dialyzer actually ran against, so moving it
# is a deploy-affecting change that belongs in its own PR and ships as a release.
elixir 1.20.4-otp-29
erlang 29.1.1
nodejs 22.23.3
